Q: Only load content when accordion is expanded - WordPress &amp; Elementor A: <p>Using <code>aria-expanded</code> or hiding the accordion content with JavaScript won't improve the initial page-load time. The 90+ forms are still rendered and downloaded by the browser.</p>
<p>A better approach is to <strong>lazy-load each Gravity Form when its accordion item is opened</strong>. Keep only the accordion title and an empty container in the initial HTML, for example:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;details class=&quot;lazy-form&quot; data-form-id=&quot;23&quot;&gt;
    &lt;summary&gt;Form 23&lt;/summary&gt;
    &lt;div class=&quot;form-container&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/details&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Then listen for the <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> <code>toggle</code> event and make an AJAX request only when the item is opened. The WordPress AJAX callback can render the requested Gravity Form with <code>gravity_form()</code>.</p>
<p>This way, the initial page contains only the accordion headings, and the form markup is generated only when the user actually opens that accordion.</p>
<p>Also, if the page can be opened directly with <code>#form-23</code>, the same lazy-load function can be called after detecting the URL hash so that the requested form is loaded automatically.</p>
